WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 4 Leader Video
Unit 1, Lesson 4: Logic

1
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.490
Now you might be asking, why in the world are we talking about logic?

2
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.810
I mean, certainly it makes sense to talk about faith.

3
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.090
Certainly it makes sense to talk about truth.

4
00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:22.330
But why would we take an entire week out to talk about something so strange in a Bible

5
00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.410
study about logic?

6
00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:30.970
Well, I think it starts with what the writer of Hebrews says in the last part of chapter

7
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.330
5 and the early part of chapter 6.

8
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:39.650
So does anyone who lives on milk, still an infant, is not acquainted with the teaching

9
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:45.130
about righteousness, but solid food is for the mature, who by constant use have trained

10
00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.010
themselves to distinguish good from evil.

11
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:55.210
Therefore, let us leave the elementary teachings about Christ and go on to maturity.

12
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:59.930
Peter says much the same thing about leaving the idea of spiritual milk and moving on

13
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:00.930
to maturity.

14
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.370
Paul says, be transformed in the renewing of your minds and put off the old self and put

15
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:10.450
on the new self, imitate God, have a mind like Christ.

16
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:18.930
And even Jesus said, we need to worship God with all of our heart, with all of our soul,

17
00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.490
with all of our mind, and with all of our strength.

18
00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:29.850
It's clear throughout Scripture that logic has a place and that is a tool that shouldn't

19
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.330
be considered a threat.

20
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.050
In fact, it's an incredibly useful tool.

21
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.730
I mean, after all, Jesus said it and in fact, you'll find the Bible study this week that

22
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.090
Jesus even demonstrated it.

23
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.010
We'll see a couple of strange things throughout the study and one of those things is this

24
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:51.050
weird term that you see in Latin called reductio ad absurdum.

25
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.410
I'll say that again, reductio ad absurdum.

26
00:01:54.410 --> 00:02:03.530
And essentially, it's just a fancy Latin word that means reduce to absurdity.

27
00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.570
It's that simple and even that seems kind of confusing.

28
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.530
So let me give you an example that should help with your students to explain this kind

29
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.530
of strange concept.

30
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:19.690
When I was a kid, ever so often I would skip school and this one time in particular

31
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.130
my dad caught me.

32
00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.370
So naturally he asks me, why did you skip school?

33
00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:31.010
I had this genius idea, this genius logic that I came up with and I told him, well, all

34
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.050
my friends were doing it so that's why I did it.

35
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.050
That makes sense.

36
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.530
That made perfect sense in my head and I kind of stood there waiting for him to make a

37
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.570
response and he didn't take very long before he said, well, if all of your friends jumped

38
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:49.290
off a bridge, would you too?

39
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:54.850
Essentially what my dad was doing, whether he realized it or not, was taking my argument,

40
00:02:54.850 --> 00:03:00.610
drawing it out to its natural conclusion, which is absurd.

41
00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.130
And that's essentially what reductio ad absurdum is telling us.

42
00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:11.570
And when we see Matthew 22 is Jesus engaging in the same sort of logical analysis in case

43
00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.170
confrontation with the Pharisees.

44
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:20.650
So instead of fearing logic, our goal is to get students to embrace logic and actually

45
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:26.210
get them to understand that logic is a vital tool in the search for truth.

46
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.570
Remember what we're doing here throughout this process and we talked about this.

47
00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:33.210
We talked about truth is that students are looking for what is true.

48
00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:38.890
We want students at the end of this process to be able to look truth squarely in the

49
00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.370
face and actually see Jesus.

50
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.370
So logic is going to help them get to that point.

51
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:50.650
So in their intro experience, we want them first to actually see that in their relationships.

52
00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:56.930
So students are using logic in their relationships to search for truth in those relationships

53
00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.850
and we want them to do the same thing with Jesus.

54
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.850
So take Paul, for example.

55
00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:06.450
If you look through Acts, whether Paul's in Corinth, whether he's in Ephesus, whether

56
00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:13.130
Paul's in Thessalonica or in Athens, what you see is that Paul is constantly using logic.

57
00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:19.050
He's reasoning from the Scriptures about helping them understand who Jesus is, what he's all

58
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.330
about, what salvation means.

59
00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:27.330
Essentially Paul uses logic through and through in almost every single one of his arguments

60
00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.690
as he's traveling on his missionary journeys.

61
00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:36.330
So the idea is we want to invite students into a space where they can be curious and

62
00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:41.930
not only ask the questions but sort of logically think through these things and add an intellectual

63
00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.770
piece to their faith.

64
00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:49.770
So as you dive into small group, you're going to ask what looks like a really simple question.

65
00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:50.970
Should faith make sense?

66
00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.610
That's kind of tricky, right?

67
00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.490
There's a little bit going on here that we need to address because there's so

68
00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.490
much about Jesus that does not seem to make sense.

69
00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:04.010
There's so much about our faith that doesn't seem to make sense sometimes.

70
00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:10.330
But as we think through, as we use logic, as we actually engage intellectually with our

71
00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:15.850
faith, there's a lot we can figure out and God has given us the tools to do so.

72
00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.450
And really this is much of the Pharisees' problem, this is what we see from our

73
00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:23.890
question one and their motivation to get after Jesus.

74
00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.890
And they just didn't really get it.

75
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:31.810
But I want to take a second and really touch on question three because this deals with potential

76
00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.530
arguments against Christianity and whether or not these arguments make sense.

77
00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:41.770
Be careful here but answer these honestly or talk about these honestly because there

78
00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:47.610
are some arguments out there that sound pretty good that seem to make some sense.

79
00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.890
But that doesn't mean that they're logical and doesn't make them right.

80
00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:52.650
It just makes them additional arguments.

81
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.970
But a lot of them are worth talking about.

82
00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:58.810
But make sure, gauge your students, make sure you know where they're at.

83
00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:02.530
If you don't think your students can handle it, then that may be a question you might want

84
00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.130
to sidestep for just a minute and then come back to it when you think that they're

85
00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:07.130
ready.

86
00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:11.490
So as students depart for this week, leave them with this question.

87
00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.650
What about your faith doesn't make sense to you?

88
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:20.970
And challenge them with what it's going to take to spur them on to a mature faith.

89
00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:27.610
Because remember, Jesus calls us all to clear and careful thinking, the same kind of thinking

90
00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:28.690
that Jesus demonstrated.

